Correct
A procurement team stores supplier master data in Azure SQL, and that system must remain the system of record. Users need to see current supplier rows inside a model-driven app and use them in Dataverse forms without building a scheduled synchronization process. Data duplication must be minimized.



Which approach should you use?

A. Standard table with scheduled sync

Explanation
A standard table with scheduled sync would require building a synchronization process to regularly update the supplier master data in the model-driven app. This approach would involve duplicating the data and may not minimize data duplication as required.
B. Elastic table with plug-in sync

Explanation
An elastic table with plug-in sync would also involve building a synchronization process to keep the supplier master data updated in the model-driven app. This approach does not eliminate the need for a scheduled synchronization process and may lead to data duplication.
C. Custom connector in canvas app

Explanation
Using a custom connector in a canvas app would allow users to access the supplier master data from Azure SQL directly, but it would not eliminate the need for a synchronization process to keep the data current in the model-driven app. This approach may still lead to data duplication.
Your answer is correct
D. Virtual table

Explanation
A virtual table allows users to access and interact with the supplier master data in Azure SQL directly within the model-driven app without the need for a scheduled synchronization process. This approach minimizes data duplication by ensuring that users are always viewing the most up-to-date supplier rows without the need for manual synchronization.
Overall explanation
A virtual table is the best answer because the external system must remain the source of truth and the data needs to appear in Dataverse-driven experiences without copying it into native Dataverse storage. That is the exact design space for virtual tables: surfacing external data as Dataverse tables while avoiding replication.



The other options all introduce the wrong tradeoff. Standard and elastic tables would move the design toward persisted Dataverse storage plus synchronization logic, which the scenario explicitly tries to avoid. A connector can call the external system, but it does not model that external data as Dataverse table data within the model-driven experience in the same way a virtual table does.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• A. Standard table with scheduled sync. This would duplicate the supplier data into Dataverse and add synchronization complexity that the scenario explicitly says to avoid. It can work technically, which makes it a plausible distractor, but it violates the requirement that Azure SQL remain the master with minimal duplication. It also creates ongoing concerns around latency, conflict handling, and sync failure recovery. The question is testing when not to materialize external data into a standard table.



• B. Elastic table with plug-in sync. Elastic tables are for very large-scale, high-throughput storage patterns, not for representing externally mastered line-of-business reference data inside model-driven forms. This option also still depends on synchronizing rows into Dataverse, which is the wrong architectural direction here. The plug-in wording makes it sound more sophisticated, but it adds code and operational overhead without solving the core requirement. High scale is not the deciding factor in this scenario; external mastery and no duplication are.



• C. Custom connector in canvas app. A custom connector is useful when you need to call a REST or SOAP API from Power Apps or Power Automate. However, the requirement is to surface supplier data as table-like data inside a model-driven app experience, not just invoke an API from a custom screen. This option is also weaker because it changes the interaction pattern from native Dataverse-style table access to explicit connector-based calls. It is close enough to tempt someone thinking “external system means connector,” but it is still the wrong fit.

