• A. Dataverse row ownership. This is a strong indicator that the data should live as native Dataverse records, especially when ownership, security, and record-centric behavior are central. A connector can retrieve or send data, but it does not make the external system participate as true Dataverse-owned business data. This makes the option plausible because external data can still be shown in apps, but it fails the governance and data model expectation in the question. When ownership matters, you usually want a table decision, not a direct connector decision.



• D. Complex relational reporting inside Dataverse. This requirement points toward persisted Dataverse data, typically in standard tables, because relational reporting depends on the data being modeled and queryable inside the platform. Connector-based access is not the natural answer when the reporting experience itself is expected to be Dataverse-centric. A connector can bring in data or trigger operations, but it does not substitute for a well-modeled relational data store. This option is wrong because it describes a reason to store and model, not a reason to call externally.



