A Dataverse-triggered cloud flow updates the same account row after calculating a routing code. The team adds the following trigger condition before enabling the flow.

Snippet

@not(equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/cr6f8_processed'], true))
What is the main design purpose of this condition?

Your answer is correct
A. Prevent self-trigger recursion

Explanation
The condition @not(equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/cr6f8_processed'], true)) is designed to prevent self-trigger recursion in the Dataverse-triggered cloud flow. By checking if the 'cr6f8_processed' field is not already set to true, the condition ensures that the flow does not get triggered repeatedly for the same account row, thus preventing recursion.
B. Enforce row-level privileges

Explanation
Enforcing row-level privileges is not the main design purpose of the condition @not(equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/cr6f8_processed'], true)). This condition is specifically used to prevent self-trigger recursion in the flow and does not relate to managing or enforcing row-level privileges in the Dataverse environment.
C. Cache connector access tokens

Explanation
The condition @not(equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/cr6f8_processed'], true)) does not cache connector access tokens. This condition is focused on preventing self-trigger recursion in the flow by checking the value of the 'cr6f8_processed' field, rather than managing access tokens for connectors used in the flow.
D. Increase retry throughput

Explanation
Increasing retry throughput is not the main design purpose of the condition @not(equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/cr6f8_processed'], true)). This condition is specifically implemented to prevent self-trigger recursion in the Dataverse-triggered cloud flow, rather than optimizing the flow for increased retry throughput.
Overall explanation
This trigger condition is intended to prevent the flow from repeatedly triggering itself after it updates the same Dataverse row. If the flow writes back to the row and marks cr6f8_processed as true, future updates that match that state will not trigger another run. That is a common design pattern for avoiding recursion or endless reprocessing in Dataverse-triggered cloud flows.



The key design idea is that the condition filters execution before the flow begins running. That is much cleaner than letting every update start a run and then trying to exit later with branch logic. In automation design, especially when the same record is updated by the flow, preventing unnecessary invocations at the trigger level improves reliability, reduces noise, and lowers the risk of loops.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• B. Enforce row-level privileges. Trigger conditions do not implement Dataverse security trimming or privilege enforcement. Security is handled by the connection identity and the permissions assigned to that identity. This expression simply evaluates incoming trigger data to decide whether the flow should start. It is easy to confuse control logic with security logic, but they solve different problems.



• C. Cache connector access tokens. Access token management is handled by the connection and underlying authentication framework, not by a trigger condition expression. This expression reads a Dataverse column value and performs a Boolean check. It has nothing to do with OAuth token storage or reuse. The option sounds technical, but it is unrelated to what the snippet is doing.



• D. Increase retry throughput. Retry behavior is configured on supported actions and connectors, not implemented through a trigger condition like this one. The expression does not change retry policy, parallelism, or backoff settings. Its purpose is filtering, not performance tuning. It may indirectly reduce load by skipping runs, but that is not the primary design intent.

