An automated cloud flow reserves inventory in an external API whenever an order row is added to Dataverse. During peak imports, duplicate reservations appear because multiple runs process overlapping updates before the external system can complete its reservation, and the API is not idempotent.



What should you change first?

A. Increase retry count

Explanation
Increasing the retry count may help in handling failed API calls, but it will not prevent the issue of duplicate reservations caused by overlapping updates during peak imports. It is not the most effective solution to address the root cause of the problem.
B. Add business rule

Explanation
Adding a business rule may help in enforcing certain conditions or validations on the data, but it will not directly solve the issue of duplicate reservations caused by overlapping updates. It is not the most appropriate action to take as the first step to address the problem.
Your answer is correct
C. Limit trigger concurrency

Explanation
Limiting trigger concurrency can help in controlling the number of simultaneous runs of the cloud flow, ensuring that only one instance processes updates at a time. This can prevent overlapping updates and reduce the occurrence of duplicate reservations during peak imports, making it the most suitable change to implement first.
D. Enable child flow retries

Explanation
Enabling child flow retries may help in handling failures within the flow execution, but it will not address the issue of duplicate reservations caused by overlapping updates. It is not the most effective solution to prevent the problem from occurring during peak imports.
Overall explanation
Limiting trigger concurrency is the best first design change because the stated failure pattern is caused by overlapping flow runs against a downstream system that cannot safely handle parallel duplicate requests. Reducing the number of simultaneous trigger executions helps serialize processing and prevents multiple runs from racing through the same reservation logic at the same time. That addresses the design flaw most directly.



This does not magically make a non-idempotent external API perfect, but it is the most appropriate first correction based on the evidence in the scenario. A retry change could make duplicates worse, a business rule would not govern external call concurrency, and child flow retries do not solve contention at the trigger level. In cloud-flow design, concurrency control is often the first lever when duplicate downstream effects are caused by parallel execution rather than by bad business data.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• A. Increase retry count. Increasing retries can be useful for transient failures, but it does not address the root issue described here. In fact, if the external API is already producing duplicate side effects, adding more retries can amplify the problem. The scenario points to overlapping parallel execution, not to temporary connectivity errors. This option is plausible because retries are a common resilience tool, but it is the wrong first change.



• B. Add business rule. A business rule operates in the app layer for validation or client/server rule behavior, not for governing cloud-flow runtime concurrency against an external API. It does not serialize flow runs or manage downstream reservation timing. The incident is integration-related and execution-related, not a form-rule design issue. This makes the option feel Power Platform-relevant while still being the wrong component for the problem.



• D. Enable child flow retries. Retry settings on a child flow do not solve the parent trigger’s parallel execution pattern. Multiple parent runs can still invoke the same child flow concurrently and create the same duplicate side effect. The scenario requires control earlier in the pipeline, at the point where the event-driven automation fans out. This option sounds close because it stays inside Power Automate, but it misses the actual location of the design problem.

